  "text_md": ";\n\n![](<11_pg145_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<11_pg145_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n# PER 617 PER\n\nPERQUIRE. To search into. Clobery's Divine\n\nForby, ii. 249.\n\nGl. u. 38 ; Wilbraham, p. 107 ;\n\nTo perk one's self up, to adorn. To perk up again, to recover from sickness.\n\nGlimpses, 1659, p. 73.\n\n- PERR. (1) Perry. (2) A pearl. PERRE. A dish in old cookery, made chiefly\n\nof peas, onions, and spices.\n\nPERRIER. A kind of short mortar, formerly\n\nmuch used for stone shot.\n\nPERRIWINKLE. A periwig. Stubbe. PERRONENDERE. A pardoner. Heame.\n\nPERRY-DANCERS. The aurora borealis. East\n\nPERRYE. (1) A squall.\n\nIt happened Harold his sonne to arrive at Pountiou against his will, by occasion of a sudden perry, or contrarie winde, that arose while he was on\n\nseaboorde. Lambnrde's Perambulation, 1596, p. 357,\n\n- (2) A httle cur dog. North.\n- (3) Precious stones\n\n\n;\n\njewels. {A.-N.)\n\nAnd alle was set with perrye,\n\nTher was never no better in Crystyant^.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ft. U.a8,f. 242\n\n- PERS. (1) Persia. We woot bothe bi story and vers That the kyndam of Grace and Pera Were hede kyngus in forme tide.\n\n\n- (4) A perch. Suffolk. \"Ov-^t the perke to pryk,\" Skelton, i. 124. It also occurs in Reliq. Antiq. i. 294.\n- (5) A wooden frame against which sawn timber is set up to dry. East.\n\n\nPERKERS. Young rooks. North.\n\nPERKIN. Water cyder.\n\nPERKY. Saucy ; obstinate. West.\n\n## PERLATANE.\n\nThe hauUe also of this palace was sett fulle of ymagez of golde, and bitwix thame stode pei-latanea\n\nof golde, in the branches of whilke ther were many maners of fewles. MS. Lincoln A. i. 17, f. 25.\n\nPERLESY. A pleurisy.\n\nAnd smyttSs hym als it were with a perlesy, that\n\nalle his lymes dryes, that he may na gud do als he sulde. MS. Lincoln A. i. 17. f. 246.\n\nPERLID. Ornamented with pearls ; studded\n\nwith any ornaments.\n\nAnd many a perZtdgarnement\n\nEmbroudid was ajen the day. Gower, MS. Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 54.\n\nCurso)- Mundi, MS. Coll. Trin. Cantab, f. 132.\n\n- (2) Company. Al we wite it thi defaut. So siggeth al our pera.\n\nArthour and Merlin, p. 9.\n\n- (3) Sky, or blueish gray colour. There was a kind of cloth so called.\n\n\nPERLIN. The piece of timber which runs along under the middle part of the spars or bearers of a roof, to give such bearers addi-\n\ntional strength.\n\nPERLOWES. PerUous. PaUgrave.\n\nPERMAFAY. By my faith. {A.-N.)\n\nPERSAUNT. Piercing. {A.-N.)\n\nPERMANSIE. Magic ; necromancy.\n\nThat of the stremis every maner wy3te\n\nPERN. (1) To prosper. Somerset.\n\nAstonied was, they weren so bryjte and shene. Ant to the ye for peraaunt for to sene.\n\n(2) To pick and dress birds, particularly applied\n\nLydgate, MS. Sue. Antiq. 134, f. 23.\n\nto dressing the heron.\n\nFor thy perseynt charit^.\n\nPERNASO. Mount Parnassus.\n\nGowei; MS. Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 109.\n\nPERNEL. The pimpernel, a flower that always\n\nPERSCRUTE. To search through. {Lat.) Used\n\nshuts up its blossoms before rain.\n\nby Andrew Borde, Brit. Bibl. iv. 24.\n\nBut these tender pemels must have one gown for the day, another for the night.\n\nPERSE. Equality. (A.-N.)\n\nPERSEL. Parsley. Pegge.\n\n- Pilkington'a Workt, p. 56.\n\nPERSEVER. To persevere. Shak.\n\nPERPEND. To consider attentively.\n\nWhether a daw sit, or whether a daw fly.\n\nYou'll quickly know, if you do well perpend.\n\nWhether a daw stand, or whether a daw lye. Whether a daw crecpe, or whether a daw cry, In what case soever a daw persever,\n\nAnd observe rightly what's the proper end.\n\nBrome'a Songs, 1661, p. 182.\n\nPERPENDICLE. The plumb line of a quadrant. This word occurs in an old treatise on\n\nA daw is a daw, and a daw shall be ever.\n\nTarlton'a Jeats, 1611.\n\nmensuration, in MS. Sloane213.\n\nPERSIAN-WHEEL. An engine invented to raise a quantity of water sufficient for overflowing lands, that border in the banks of rivers, where the streams lie so low, as to be incapable of doing it.\n\nPERPENTINE. A porcupine. \" Perpoynt, %*trix\" Pr. Parv. The formperpenfine occurs\n\nin Shakespeare, most incorrectly altered to porcupine by modern editors. It is the\n\ngenuine old word.\n\nPERSON. A mask, or actor. {Lat.)\n\nPERPENT-STONE. A large stone reaching\n\nPERSONABLE. Personally visible.\n\nthrough a wall so as to appear on both sides of it. Oxf. Gl. Arch. p. 280. In the North of England, a thin wall, the stones of which are built on the edge, is called a perpent.\n\nMy saied lorde of Winchester saied unto the kyng that the kyng his father, so visited with sitkenesse, was not personable. Hall, Henry VI. f. 13.\n\nPERSONE. A man. Generally, a man of\n\nPERPETUANA. A kind of glossy cloth, gene-\n\ndignity, a parson or rector of a church.\n\nPERSORE. A piercing-iron.\n\nrally called everlasting.\n\nPERPLANTED. Planted securely.\n\n3e, je, seyd the persore. That at I sey it shall be sure\n\nRequlrynge theim as his especiall truste and confidence was perplanted in the hope of their fidelite, that they would occurrc and mete hym by the vraye with all diligent preparacion.\n\nWhi chyd je iche one with other ? Wote 56 wele I ame jour brother ! Therefore none contrary me.\n\nFore as I sey so schall it be. MS.Ashmole Cl>\n\nHall, Richard lU. f. 27.\n\n",
